_book_page_[2] = 
    '<h1 class="c4" id="h.sc3djr98on61"><span class="c3 c11">PROLOGUE</span></h1><h2 class="c4" id="h.dxysgd2t59kt"><span class="c3 c7">The death</span></h2><p class="c5 c9"><span class="c3 c8"></span></p><p class="c5"><span class="c0">Early morning. Three people were standing in front of the club. Two of them were elves. One was </span><span class="c2 c10">Orkan Scharfsinning</span><span class="c0">, a fair-skinned blond with sharp green eyes and long pointed ears. A tight black bodysuit hugged his narrow frame, while loose cargo pants with far too many pockets hung from his hips. The other elf, </span><span class="c2 c10">Wolfram</span><span class="c0">, had a warmer skin tone, silver hair and glasses. The third member of the group was a tall, muscular girl with greenish skin, short brown hair and visible tusks. At first glance, there was no mistaking her for anything but an orc. Her name was </span><span class="c2 c10">Brief Scuffs</span><span class="c0 c3">.</span></p><p class="c5 c9"><span class="c0 c3"></span></p><p class="c5"><span class="c0">They had just arrived. Brief was looking up, scanning the airspace for guard drones.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">All seems to be clear.</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; She gave the sky one more glance before turning her head to the boys. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">Orkan?</span><span class="c2 c10"><br></span><span class="c0">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Yeah.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Orkan was fiddling with his cyberdeck, his tone full of annoyance. He was busy checking the building plan for the tenth time and didn&rsquo;t want anyone to bother him.<br>&mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">I&rsquo;m sure you&rsquo;ve memorized it enough.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief gave him a small smile, yet she seemed a little frustrated. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Now, can you give the deck to Wolfram? We still need to hack the cameras.<br></span><span class="c0">&mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Right.</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Orkan rolled his eyes with an annoyed sigh, after which he finally handed the cyberdeck to Wolfram. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">There.<br></span><span class="c0">Wolfram took the device without much gratitude on his face.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Thank you.</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; He quickly started typing something, and after a mere minute he gave the deck to Brief.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Gorgeous work, buddy.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; At these words from Brief, Wolfram blushed slightly, but she didn&rsquo;t notice. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Alri-i-ight, everything seems to be okay now. Let&rsquo;s repeat our plan just one more time before moving out, shall we?<br></span><span class="c0">Orkan let out an unhappy sigh. He didn&rsquo;t need to repeat the plan for the hundredth time in a row. All he wanted right now was to finish the gig as soon as possible and get paid. He was short on money and the rent deadline was getting closer&hellip;<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Don&rsquo;t make that face, it doesn&rsquo;t make you look smarter, you know.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram glanced at Orkan before turning his attention back to Brief.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">So, what we need is to steal a diamond Anubis statuette from Edward&rsquo;s office. That&rsquo;d be suicidal if he was here, but he should be on the other side of the city today, so we&rsquo;re kind of safe.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief gestured vaguely towards the building. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">So that&rsquo;s good. The plan is, Orkan and Wolfram are going straight for the office through the vents and I&#39;ll try to sneak there through the halls and maintenance rooms&hellip; And I&#39;ll let you know if something unexpected happens down there. Just tell me your earphones are ready and we can start.<br></span><span class="c0">&mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">All good.</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Wolfram gave Brief a thumbs up.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I don&rsquo;t know why we had to waste so much time repeating the same thing again.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Orkan remained silent for about two seconds before speaking again. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">The earphone&rsquo;s working. Unlike us right now&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; He said the last words quietly enough for his teammates not to hear them.<br>Brief walked closer to the wall where the closed vent was located. She was tall enough that reaching its cover wasn&rsquo;t a problem, and after a couple of seconds she ripped it off completely, revealing the tunnel inside.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I can&rsquo;t say I envy you guys, haha.</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; She smiled at the boys and tossed the vent cover aside. She was too broad to fit into the tunnel herself, otherwise she&rsquo;d most likely have gone that way as well. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Okay, Orkan, you go first.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Hhmmgfh&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Orkan wrinkled his nose in utter disgust at what was to come&hellip; He hated the whole idea of crawling through the vent, collecting dirt on his expensive clothes and breathing in the dust. How humiliating&hellip; Especially for someone with such a rich and influential past!<br>One light punch in the back from Wolfram snapped Orkan out of his thoughts.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Come on already, you&rsquo;re just slowing us down.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram walked closer to the wall, looking up at the vent. Both elves were too short to reach it without help.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Ah great, I guess we have to find a ladder somewhe-</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Orkan&rsquo;s rant was quickly cut off when Brief grabbed him around the waist, lifted him into the air and shoved him into the ventilation tunnel.<br>&mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Move! C&rsquo;mon Wolfie, you&#39;re next.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; She said it with audible impatience.<br>Without any other choice, Orkan had to start moving forward, crawling through the dirty and dusty vent. A couple of seconds later Wolfram was following him, leaving Brief behind.<br>The farther they went, the more dust coated the walls of the ventilation shaft and the harder it became to breathe. Elves were well known for their poor health, so after several minutes of crawling neither of them felt particularly good.<br>Yet Orkan wasn&rsquo;t used to the physical strain of mercenary work, so he felt even worse than his comrade.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Pffth&hellip; Eugh&hellip;</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; He coughed, trying to be as quiet as possible.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Shush!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram was managing to hold back his own coughs and keep quiet, and Orkan&rsquo;s carelessness was obviously getting on his nerves.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I&#39;m trying!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; While saying these words, Orkan inhaled even more dust, which only made him cough harder.<br>He had to stop just to let himself cough and wheeze, his head spinning from the lack of fresh air. Unfortunately, Wolfram didn&rsquo;t manage to stop in time and bumped straight into his teammate&rsquo;s back.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Oh come on!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Not particularly happy about running face-first into someone&rsquo;s rear, Wolfram raised his voice slightly. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">We have work to do!<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I can&#39;t breathe!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Orkan hissed, trying his best to calm the coughing fit as his eyes started watering from the effort.<br>Eventually, both of them had to silence themselves completely when someone spoke below.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Did you hear something?</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; The voice was low, most likely coming from some big guy.<br>&mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">I&#39;m not sure?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The second voice was female, hoarse and tired. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Must&#39;ve been the VIPs? These fuckers always do all types of shit behind closed doors, y&rsquo;know.<br></span><span class="c0">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Probably.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The man sounded like he had just shrugged. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">I wish I could get a little taste of those &ldquo;types of shit&rdquo;, you have no idea how tired I am of patrolling these stupid halls for god knows how long.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Well, this place has a lot of girls to choose from, why not spend some time with one or two on your days off?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The woman didn&rsquo;t seem particularly interested in the conversation though.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Nah. They all know I&#39;m an asshole, they wouldn&#39;t agree even for millions of bucks.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The man sighed. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Gotta return to patrolling, the halls aren&#39;t going to check themselves. See you later.<br></span><span class="c0">The conversation ended, but the two elves stayed silent and still for another minute, making sure no more noises came from below.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Told you.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0 c3">&mdash; Wolfram pushed Orkan in the back, hinting that he should finally move.<br>With that, they continued navigating the dusty ventilation tunnels, heading for the club owner&rsquo;s office. If only the air there were more breathable&hellip;</span></p><hr><p class="c5"><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I hope they&#39;ll be alright.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief sighed heavily as the two elves disappeared into the darkness of the vent.<br>She shook her head, gathering her thoughts. She had arguably the hardest job of them all, getting into the office while staying hidden from security&hellip; She could always brute-force her way in, but that would most likely ruin the whole mission.<br>After a couple of seconds, Brief walked through the main entrance of the club. She passed the coat check and entered the bar area. A long counter stood in the middle of the room with plenty of people sitting around it, talking, drinking&hellip; Pink and red neon lights washed over the room, giving it an intimate atmosphere, especially combined with the marble statues of half-naked men and women.<br>&ldquo;Red Roses&rdquo; definitely wasn&rsquo;t a club meant for broke people like Brief. Still, she was there, and she didn&rsquo;t have any time to stand around and enjoy the scenery. She needed to get to the third floor as soon as possible.<br>The orc paced through the room while cautiously looking around, searching for one particular door that led to the stairwell. Like other orcs, she didn&rsquo;t have the excellent memory many other species did, yet she had studied the building plan so many times that by now it felt printed into her brain. A couple of seconds later she spotted the door, with a security guard standing right in front of it. The man was human, yet he had so much chrome in him that he looked more like some strange machine&hellip; Obviously, she wasn&rsquo;t going to ask him to let her through. That would&rsquo;ve been too naive and blatantly stupid. Brief had to find a better way past him&hellip;<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Yes, that could work&hellip;</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; She murmured to herself while side-eyeing two obviously drunk men at the bar counter.<br>With an idea in mind, Brief waited for both men to look away from her, then gave one of them a deliberately rude shove in the shoulder.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Errn?</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; The man who had been pushed looked around, but Brief had already stepped away. The closest person to him was the other drunk guy. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">Ayy&hellip; Did you motherfucker just push me?<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">The fuck are you talking ab&hellip; About, eh? Are you too drunk or somthnm&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The second man faced the first one, a challenge in his eyes.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I am sober enough to not&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The first man hiccuped. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">&hellip;To not push people around like they&#39;re some motherfucking eh&hellip; These things&hellip; You get it!<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">You&#39;re trying to say what&hellip; That you&#39;re a fucking victim here!?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The second man got out of his seat, his legs wobbling.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">It&#39;s not me who behaves like a total asshole!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; With that, the first man got to his feet as well, his stance slightly less unstable.<br>Brief took a few more steps back, watching the argument unfold from a safe distance. It wasn&rsquo;t even five seconds before the two men started fighting, both too drunk to understand who had actually caused their conflict.<br>Eventually, just as Brief intended, the security guard noticed the fight and rushed over to remove both of them from the club. Obviously, fighting wasn&rsquo;t allowed in a place this prestigious.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">You two! The hell is going on?!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The guard stopped right in front of the fighting men and pulled them apart.<br>At the same time, while everyone was too busy watching the fight, Brief slipped into the stairwell, which was now completely unguarded. She didn&rsquo;t have any time to waste. The floors ahead weren&rsquo;t meant for outsiders like her.<br>Brief decided to contact her teammates while she was alone to check how they were doing and what was happening up there.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Hey, it&#39;s me. How&#39;s it going on your end?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; She whispered, glancing around restlessly.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Managing.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram replied, his voice slightly hoarse. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Still on the second floor right now.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Got you. How many security guards are there?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief sighed with a hint of relief. She was glad to hear her teammates were okay.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">There are some. Be cautious, okay?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram let out a small, worrying cough.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I will.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; She clicked a small button on her earphone to mute herself, ending the conversation.<br>Slowly, Brief started creeping up the stairs, constantly checking for anyone nearby. Despite the tense situation, she kept herself collected and cool-headed. She knew that panicking would only lead to reckless decisions, and that was the last thing she needed&hellip;<br>The stairs ended. Brief peeked into the hall, checking whether anyone was around. It seemed clear for now, so after waiting another moment she started sneaking forward. The second floor was reserved for VIP guests and filled with doors decorated by glowing neon hearts&hellip; Yeah, it was obvious what exactly those VIPs did here. Even though the walls and doors seemed to be soundproof, Brief was sure she could still hear muffled moans from behind some of them. It was annoying&hellip; And distracting. The entire floor also smelled of overly sweet floral candles.<br>A loud metallic clank came from the end of the hall. Brief knew it was one of the Red Roses security guards. The owner of the place, Edward Kitt, hired human cyborgs in his clubs on principle. He was well known for helping fellow humans in the city by giving them jobs and necessary chrome&hellip;<br>Brief could respect that, but she wasn&rsquo;t particularly happy to hear one of them right now. She quickly scanned the hall and noticed a slightly open door&hellip; It wasn&rsquo;t the best option, but there was nowhere else to hide, so she slipped into the room.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Who- Who are you?!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; A female voice called out as Brief closed the door.<br>The orc spun around and saw a large white wolf lying on the bed, obviously a werewolf.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Oh I&#39;m- I&#39;m so sorry! I didn&#39;t know anyone was there!</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Brief gave a genuinely apologetic bow. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">I&#39;m quite in a situation right now, please don&#39;t call security!<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Why shouldn&#39;t I?!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The wolfess grabbed a blanket with her teeth and pulled it over herself. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">What&#39;s going on?! Are you some kind of thief or a burglar, why are you running into someone&#39;s rooms?!<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">The door was open, I thought the room was empty, please forgive me.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief made another small apologetic bow. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">But I really need to stay here, just for a little bit&hellip;<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">It was open? Oh. I didn&#39;t realise that, I guess. It&#39;s hard to close the doors when you have paws.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The werewolf&rsquo;s tone softened when she realised that the open door had been her own fault. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Mm&hellip; So, what are you doing here anyways? Why the rush?<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I&#39;d say it&#39;s a secret.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The orc pressed her ear to the door, trying to hear what was happening outside. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Just a mercenary gig.</span><span class="c2 c10"><br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">So you are a thief? Or a killer? Or what?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The wolfess wagged her tail with interest.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I can&#39;t&hellip; Not right now?</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Brief narrowed her eyes, focusing on the sounds beyond the door.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Trying to understand if security is still there, aren&#39;t you?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The werewolf woman gave her a sly grin, crossing her paws.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Mhm&hellip;</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Brief only nodded in response.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Nobody&#39;s there anymore, he walked away.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The wolfess made an innocent face when Brief looked at her with suspicion. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">What? Don&#39;t tell me you think your hearing is better than mine. Mmm&hellip; I&#39;d want just one thing as a &ldquo;thank you&rdquo; for my help.<br></span><span class="c0">Brief raised an eyebrow questioningly.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Ugh, I hate my boyfriend Brian, he&#39;s such a bastard, but&hellip; I love him, you know?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The werewolf rolled her eyes, yet her tail kept wagging. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">I was thinking maybe someone could tell him I&#39;m dead? Or something like this, just to see if he even cares about me&hellip; Ughh, I don&#39;t care if it&#39;ll be you or someone else, just tell anyone I need a merc, I want to know if he loves me as much as I love him.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Uhh&hellip; Alright, of course. Just tell me your number or your email address or&hellip; Anything.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief shrugged. It was a fair price for the help.<br>Less than a minute later, she had the werewolf&rsquo;s email and her name, Rawrenn, and it was finally time to continue with the gig.<br>Brief opened the door by an inch, peeking outside to make sure nobody was around. With the way clear, she entered the empty hallway and silently closed the door behind her. This time she moved a little faster, eager to finally reach the third floor&hellip; Brief wanted to contact her teammates, but this wasn&rsquo;t the best place for it, so she jogged towards the next stairwell door, trying to be as quiet as her large frame allowed.<br>Luckily, nobody was in the stairwell, so the orc took a moment to contact Wolfram&hellip; And Orkan, though he wasn&rsquo;t much of a talker anyway.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Hey, I&#39;m on my way to the third floor, where are you?</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Brief leaned against the wall, gathering her thoughts.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Almost there.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram sounded even more hoarse than he had in their previous call.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Gorgeous. You can wait for me in the office, I&#39;ll be there in a couple of minutes.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; She smiled. The thought of the mission being over soon was nice.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Roger.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; With that, Wolfram ended the call.<br>Brief sighed and made her way upstairs, her mood significantly better than it had been half an hour ago. As usual, she looked around the hall and found no one there. How lucky! The orc went straight for the office. She knew exactly where it was.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Excuse me.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Someone placed a hand on Brief&rsquo;s shoulder. A cold, metal one.<br>She turned around to see a security cyborg. He had probably just stepped out of a nearby room.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">You shouldn&#39;t be here.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; He squeezed Brief&#39;s shoulder. Thanks to his massive leg implants, he was almost as tall as she was.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Agh, fuck.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief thought for a second, then punched the cyborg in the gut. The blow would&rsquo;ve sent a regular person flying across the room, but this wasn&rsquo;t a regular person.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">A feisty one, huh?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The guard only sneered in her face as he reached for a small walkie-talkie. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">An intruder is on the third floor!<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">No!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief gasped.<br>With all her strength, she struck the hand holding the walkie-talkie. The device flew into the wall and shattered into pieces. The cyborg snorted. They both knew it was already too late. He threw a powerful jab into Brief&#39;s jaw, making her gasp and recoil as blood ran from her lip. She shook her head to clear it and swept one leg at his feet.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Grh!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; He staggered but managed to stay upright, his eyes filling with anger. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">The reinforcements are on their way, you can fight as much as you want but it wouldn&#39;t help you.<br></span><span class="c0">The cyborg recovered his balance and kicked Brief hard in the chest. The impact knocked all the air out of her lungs and sent her to the floor, gasping.<br>At that moment, a couple more people entered the hall. Two, no, three of them&hellip; Brief narrowed her eyes, her vision slightly blurry from the pain in her chest.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Am I missing the fun?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; A low, calm voice spoke as its owner walked past the security guards. It belonged to a tall, muscular black man with long pink dreadlocks, a dark tailored suit and a large scar across his face. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Owie, an orc girl, how unfortunate.<br></span><span class="c0">Brief had to blink a couple of times to clear her vision because she wasn&rsquo;t sure if it really was him&hellip; </span><span class="c2 c10">Edward!</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;The owner of this club, one of the most powerful people in this damn city! They had been sure he wouldn&rsquo;t be here, but&hellip; She had to warn her teammates somehow, as soon as possible!<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Nah, don&#39;t worry, nobody&#39;s gonna hurt you this time. You know how many people are sneaking in my clubs to steal my stuff? Huh, these chromeheads catch a new one every week.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Edward smirked, though not in a particularly malicious way. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">Get her out of the club and search for the others if she works with a team.<br></span><span class="c0">Two cyborgs grabbed Brief by the arms and started walking her towards the exit.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">But be more careful next time, if I or these guys catch you again you may return home in a wooden casket. If you really want to steal my stuff you have to be more skilled than that.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0 c3">&mdash; Edward clapped his hands and winked at Brief before she was dragged away.</span></p><hr><p class="c5"><span class="c0">After some more crawling, Orkan stopped, making Wolfram almost bump into him again.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">What this time?</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Wolfram frowned, adjusting his glasses.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">We&#39;re here.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Orkan was looking down through the ventilation cover directly above Edward&#39;s office.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Finally, get that cover off then.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram tried to look past Orkan, but there wasn&rsquo;t much room.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">With what, may I ask?</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Orkan rolled his eyes.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">With a screwdriver that is in your pocket, blockhead.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram snorted. He knew Orkan just didn&rsquo;t want to do anything and was acting stupid on purpose.<br>With a heavy sigh, Orkan finally pulled out a screwdriver and started removing the bolts from the ventilation cover. It didn&rsquo;t take long before the cover came loose.<br>Orkan wasn&rsquo;t exactly sure how he was supposed to get down, but with Wolfram stuck behind him, he had no choice but to go first. He tried to carefully crawl out of the vent, yet gravity quickly took over and he fell with a loud thud.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">OW!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Orkan hissed in pain after landing directly on his right elbow.<br>Wolfram only shook his head as he made his own way out. He was completely sure he could do it better somehow, but in the end he dropped down just like Orkan and landed badly on his shoulder.<br>Half a minute later, Wolfram managed to get up, rubbing his injured shoulder.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Get up, we just have to&hellip;</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; He wrinkled his nose in pain. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">To find the statuette. It should be somewhere around.<br></span><span class="c0">Wolfram started pacing around the office, scanning the place for the wanted trophy. Orkan followed soon after, looking for the treasure as well.<br>They searched everywhere, in the drawers, on the shelves and inside the cabinets, but it didn&rsquo;t seem to be anywhere&hellip;<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Found it!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Orkan raised an arm with the Anubis statuette in it. Its perfect diamond edges caught the light and scattered it across the room.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Great!</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Wolfram rushed over, gazing at the treasure in awe. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">No wonder the client wants it so much, it&#39;s a masterpiece! A relic!<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Yeah&hellip; But I&#39;ve had a ton of even better ones in my house.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Orkan shrugged, fighting the urge to simply run off with the statuette rather than give it to anyone else.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Uh, okay.</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Wolfram wasn&rsquo;t sure how he was supposed to respond to that.<br>A second later, the office door opened. Three people stood behind it, Edward and two security guards.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Oop, I guess we&#39;ve found the rest of the team.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Edward smiled playfully. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">Ah, I see, the Anubis statuette? It&#39;s good, isn&rsquo;t it?<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">It&hellip; It&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram stuttered in shock. Why the hell hadn&rsquo;t Brief warned them HE was there?!<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Yep. So, you want to take it? Hehe. Right. Well it&#39;s just so unfortunate that I forgot to take the kettle off the stove! Whoopsie doopsie, I&#39;d love to give it to you but I gotta go.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Edward sighed and shook his head dramatically. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">I guess without me these guys would be able to use some force. Welp!<br></span><span class="c0">Edward left the room with a cheerful whistle, leaving the elves alone with the cyborgs. Orkan squeaked and shoved the statuette into Wolfram&#39;s hands.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Don&#39;t hurt me, it was his idea!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; He gulped in fear, trying to put a little distance between himself and Wolfram as if that could prove his innocence.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">WHAT?!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram squeezed the treasure and threw it to the floor in panic. The diamond shattered into countless glittering pieces, covering the carpet with sharp fragments.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Don&#39;t care.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; One of the cyborgs raised a massive plasma gun. The weapon hummed as it began heating rapidly.<br>Orkan raised his hands in surrender, inching closer and closer to the window while Wolfram stood frozen in shock. The hum of the plasma gun grew louder, and Orkan decided to make one final move. He rushed towards the open window.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">ORKAN, NO!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram shouted, stepping towards his retreating teammate.<br>The plasma ray sliced through Orkan&rsquo;s body like a hot knife through butter, splitting him cleanly in two. He didn&rsquo;t even have a moment to process the pain. Both halves simply collapsed onto the floor, exposing muscle, organs and bone. The carpet was soaked with blood within seconds.<br>Wolfram couldn&rsquo;t say a word. He had never seen a dead person before, especially not in such a gruesome state.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Wolfram, Orkan, get out of there right now! Edward is there, they just caught me!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief&rsquo;s worried voice sounded through Wolfram&#39;s earphone.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">I KNOW! Just stand there below the office win</span><span class="c1">&mdash;</span><span class="c0"><br>Another cyborg shot Wolfram in the hand. He had probably aimed for his head and missed. Wolfram screamed in agony as the bullet tore through his flesh, blood spilling down his clothes. With no other option left, he gathered all his strength, grabbed one half of Orkan&rsquo;s body, hurled it through the window and jumped out right after it.<br>The third floor wasn&rsquo;t extremely high, but the fall was still enough to break Wolfram&rsquo;s uninjured arm. He barely stayed conscious. Adrenaline kept him awake, but it felt like hell.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Wolfram! Ork- Wh-what happened?!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief stopped in front of one and a half elves, her eyes wide in horror. She felt just like Wolfram. She had never seen anything this horrifying before.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Unnghh&hellip;</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Wolfram groaned, his eyes filling with tears from both shock and pain.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">D-d&hellip; Don&#39;t worry. Everything is alright.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief took a shaky breath.<br>She looked up, but the security guards weren&rsquo;t there anymore.<br>They probably thought that punishment was more than enough and had no reason to chase the intruders any farther. The orc pulled out her phone and started searching for something with trembling hands.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Let&#39;s go.</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; She picked up Orkan&rsquo;s half of the body, utter horror and pity written across her face.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Whh&hellip; Where to?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram whined but forced himself onto his feet, his stance unstable and both arms bleeding.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">To the mechanic.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief looked away. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">He can&#39;t&hellip; Not like this&hellip; We must at least try.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Mmh&hellip; I g&hellip; Guess so&hellip; Agh&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram let out an agonised groan.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Do you need any&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; She reached one hand towards Wolfram.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">N-no, no, I&#39;m good!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; He couldn&rsquo;t even force a smile, but he still refused any help&hellip; He didn&rsquo;t want to show weakness, not in front of her.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Alright, then let&#39;s go.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0 c3">&mdash; With that, Brief turned on her heels and hurried away, expecting Wolfram to follow.</span></p><hr><p class="c5"><span class="c0">Even though they had been running for less than five minutes, it felt like an eternity. Brief and Wolfram stopped in front of a shabby-looking building with a bright neon sign that read &ldquo;CHEAP CHROME AND COOL GUNS!&rdquo;. It didn&rsquo;t look like the best option, but this man was the only mechanic in the area they could afford.<br>Brief opened the door and entered a dimly lit room. It looked something like a reception area, but nobody was there. The orc walked closer to the lone table with a note on it, which read, &ldquo;im probably in the workshop, if you need something go downstairs and knock on the door&rdquo;.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Alright&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief glanced at Wolfram, making sure he was still alive and conscious. Yet she was too afraid to look down at the dead body in her arms&hellip;<br>She made her way downstairs to the workshop and knocked on the door, hoping the mechanic was there and would somehow be able to help.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">&iexcl;Ay caramba! One second!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; A cheerful male voice shouted.<br>Rumbling and fumbling came from behind the closed door, then something heavy crashed onto the floor.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Shit!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The person sighed in frustration.<br>Two seconds later the door opened, revealing a young man with spiky blond hair, metal limbs and a mechanical left eye. His massive mechanical hands looked like they belonged at a workbench rather than on a person.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Whoa whoa whoa, it&#39;s not a cemetery! What&#39;s up with the dead dude?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The mechanic laughed, scratching the back of his head.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Can you help him?</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Brief tried to remember the name of the person standing in front of her. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">&hellip;Connor, right?</span><span class="c2 c10"><br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Me or the dead guy?</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Connor laughed. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">S&iacute;, s&iacute;, soy Connor. So, uh, repeat again what do you want from me?<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Can you&hellip; Can you do something to revive him? Anything, I&rsquo;ll&hellip; We&rsquo;ll pay as much as needed, just&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief sighed heavily.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Ahh, first time losing a friend? Dang, that&#39;s sad, I&#39;m sorry. So&hellip; You brought me half of the dude so I could save him somehow?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Connor shrugged. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">I don&#39;t want to look mean or anything but&hellip; He&#39;s totally dead.<br></span><span class="c0">Brief let out a shaky breath, barely holding herself back from crying. She didn&rsquo;t want to accept that her friend had died, even though Orkan probably hadn&rsquo;t thought of her and Wolfram as friends at all.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Well I can TRY to do something but I REALLY can&#39;t promise anything. Bring him here I guess?</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Connor stepped out of the way, letting Brief and Wolfram come in.<br>The workshop was dirty. Thick wires sprawled across the floor and the walls were covered with all kinds of guns, most likely made by Connor himself. He helped Brief place the elf&rsquo;s half-body on a medical couch, then looked down at the corpse.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Yuck.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The mechanic made a slightly disgusted face. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">How did that even happen? Like he&#39;s split PERFECTLY in two halves, how&#39;s that possible? Was he killed by a fucking pair of scissors?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; He grinned, but after noticing Brief&rsquo;s mourning face he let out an awkward chuckle. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">Ahem, perd&oacute;name.<br></span><span class="c0">Connor took a good look at Orkan&rsquo;s insides, then walked over to what looked like a scrapyard piled into one corner of the workshop.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Well&hellip; Yeah, as I&#39;ve said I can try something, but I can&#39;t guarantee anything. He&#39;d most likely stay as dead as he is right now.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; He picked something from the pile of metal parts and twirled it in his hand.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">How much would it cost?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Brief glanced at Wolfram, then at Connor, her eyes slightly hopeful.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Uhhhhh&hellip;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; The mechanic scratched the back of his head, thinking. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">I dunno, I don&#39;t even think I&#39;ll be able to do anything so let&#39;s say it&#39;s free. If I&#39;ll be able to revive him somehow then I guess I&#39;ll just be able to call myself a necromancer and that&#39;ll be enough. You know how many new customers will this get me? Yuhh, a lot.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; He chuckled. &mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">So yeah, es gratis.<br></span><span class="c0">&mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">I will pay anyway, thank you so much for even considering trying, it&#39;s&hellip;</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Brief sniffed, and Wolfram placed his shot hand on her shoulder, gently rubbing it.<br>&mdash; </span><span class="c2">And you, need anything to be done with your hands? They don&#39;t look good, hombre.</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;&mdash; Connor smirked at Wolfram, flexing his metal arms. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">Oye, I can make em fully chromed if you don&#39;t need em anymore.<br></span><span class="c0">&mdash;</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c2">N-no! I don&#39;t want that!</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Wolfram shivered. The thought of replacing any of his limbs with metal was utterly disturbing.<br>Brief sighed and looked at her still-living elf teammate. It was time to take care of him too. The poor guy was barely staying on his feet.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">We got to go right now. Can you call me if you&#39;d be able to do something with him?</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; She gestured towards Orkan&rsquo;s corpse. &mdash; </span><span class="c2">Or if you wouldn&#39;t be able to&hellip; Call me anyway, I need to know.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Sure thing, amiga.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0">&mdash; Connor took out his phone to add Brief to his contacts.<br>After the orc gave him her number, she turned to Wolfram with a concerned expression.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Come on, you need help.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;&mdash;</span><span class="c0">&nbsp;Brief didn&rsquo;t wait for his answer before lifting him into her arms. She knew Wolfram didn&rsquo;t want to seem weak in front of her, but she couldn&rsquo;t stand watching him suffer.<br></span><span class="c1">&mdash; </span><span class="c2">Bye bye.</span><span class="c1">&nbsp;</span><span class="c0 c3">&mdash; Connor smiled and returned to inspecting Orkan&rsquo;s corpse.</span></p><p class="c5 c9"><span class="c0 c3"></span></p><p class="c5"><span class="c0">The two left the workshop, heading for the nearest hospital. Both hoped they would see Orkan alive again, even though he had been a bad friend and a terrible teammate.</span></p>';

